🛠️ Step-by-Step Implementation
For each server, perform the following commands. We will use sudo to execute administrative tasks.

1. Connect and switch to root
Log into the server from the jump host, then switch to the root user to avoid permission issues.

bash
ssh tony@stapp01
# Enter password when prompted
sudo su -
2. Create the group
This command creates the nautilus_developers group. If the group already exists, the command will return an error, but this is safe to ignore .

bash
groupadd nautilus_developers
3. Handle the user creation and group assignment
You need to check if the user mohammed exists first. The most efficient way is to attempt to create the user, and if that fails (because they already exist), add them to the group instead .

bash
# Try to create the user with the group assigned. If the user exists, run the usermod command.
useradd -G nautilus_developers mohammed 2>/dev/null || usermod -aG nautilus_developers mohammed
Note: The -a flag in usermod is crucial. It means append, ensuring you don't accidentally remove mohammed from any other groups they might belong to .

4. Verify the setup
Confirm that the user was created and is correctly associated with the new group.

bash
# Check user details and group memberships
id mohammed
groups mohammed
🔄 Repeat for Remaining Servers
After completing the steps on stapp01, exit the session and repeat the entire process for stapp02 (using steve) and stapp03 (using banner).

Once finished, the group nautilus_developers will exist on all App servers, and the user mohammed will be a member of that group on each machine.
